{
  "id": "Compound::DB00542 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Benazepril, an Ace Inhibitor, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hypertension (disease) through a series of interconnected mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Benazepril may act as a causal agent in the development of Hypertension, as it is linked to conditions such as Small Bowel Angioedema, Pemphigus, and Pulmonary Eosinophilia, all of which are associated with Quinapril, another Ace Inhibitor. These conditions, in turn, are treated for Hypertension, indicating a potential pathway where Benazepril's effects are indirectly linked to the management of hypertension. The shared ancestry of Benazepril and Quinapril, both belonging to the Ace Inhibitor class, suggests a common pharmacological mechanism, possibly involving the inhibition of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. The integration of these relationships highlights the role of Ace Inhibitors in the pathophysiology of hypertension, though the exact causal link remains partially obscured by the interplay of multiple pathways.",
